+
+#### 1. Pose sampling of Study 3, recorded
+
+*Item recorded.* RUNBOOK step 10 ran `make_pose_2026.py` without `--sample-interval`, so the MOCO
+default applies: one pose record every 8 video frames (266.9 ms, about 3.75 per second). Study 1's
+pose files have one record every 4 frames (133.5 ms; all 30 clips). The step 10 command was
+written for Study 3 without checking Study 1's files; this is the cause. All 300 Study 3 clips
+(216 and 84) use the same setting, so every Study 3 comparison is made at one sampling rate. The
+kinematic variables (standard deviations of body and face positions, hand-presence ratios) have
+the same expected values at either rate; the sparser sampling makes each utterance's estimate
+noisier, which works against detecting a kinematic effect. Comparisons of kinematic values
+between Study 1 and Study 3 are reported with this difference stated. The pose is not rerun.
+
+#### 2. The tight-frame minimum is stated in time
+
+*What it was.* Amendment 9 item 6 point 1 requires "at least 8 tight frames"; item 5 (block K)
+and item 8 use that minimum. The figure assumed Study 1's sampling, where 8 frames are about 1 s (working notes, D2:
+"at least 8 tight frames (about 1 s)").
+
+*What it becomes.* In all three places: **at least 1 s of tight frames**, that is, at least
+ceil(1000 / span) tight pose records, which is **4** records at Study 3's span of 266.9 ms. An
+utterance below the minimum is "missing" for block K (training-fold mean plus a missing indicator)
+and is not a unit of item 6 point 2 or item 8. The number of utterances below the minimum is
+reported per language.
+
+*Why.* Kept at 8 records, the minimum would have meant about 2.1 s and excluded most utterances
+(Study 1's mean utterance length is about 2.5 s), which is not what was intended.
+
+#### 3. High-dimensional sources are reduced separately (Amendment 9 item 5, Models)
+
+*What it was.* "Inside each training fold, every block is standardized and reduced by PCA to
+min(16, n_train - 1) components, so that blocks enter with equal dimension."
+
+*What it becomes.* Inside each training fold, every **source** is standardized (training-fold
+mean and standard deviation). A source with more than 16 columns is then reduced by PCA to
+min(16, n_train - 1) components: the XLM-R embedding, the TF-IDF vector, the Whisper encoder
+embedding, and the face measures of block Fa if they have more than 16 columns. A source with 16 columns or fewer enters unreduced:
+the form-class indicators, the 15 prosodic features, the kinematic variables with their missing
+indicator, the 8 framing measures, and the face-presence share and missing indicator. A block is
+the concatenation of its sources: T = XLM-R + TF-IDF + form; A = Whisper + prosody; K; Fa; F.
+The TF-IDF vectorizer is the one of Amendment 4 B2 (fitted once on all Study 3 transcription
+inputs of the five covered languages, Amendment 5), applied to the items.
+
+*Why.* Reduced as one block, the TF-IDF columns (thousands) would dominate the first components
+of T and the 4 form indicators, the strongest text cue, would be largely lost; likewise the
+Whisper columns (1,280) would swamp the 15 prosodic features in A. This would weaken the
+text-only model by construction and inflate the primary statistic AUC(T+A+V) minus AUC(T) in the
+direction of the hypothesis. Found while writing the code, before any item was judged.
+
